{
  "requirement": "An outline language can additionally supply the syntax capability used by the\nSDK's [structural search](sources.md#outline-and-search-the-available-files).\nThe operation returns matched byte ranges within the supplied source:\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait OutlineLang: Clone + Send + Sync {\n    // ...\n\n    fn search_structure(\n        &self,\n        source: &str,\n        rule: &serde_json::Map<String, serde_json::Value>,\n    ) -> Result<Vec<Span>, OutlineError>;\n\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nThe default implementation returns an unsupported-language error. Plain text\nsupplies outlining only.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately check every obligation in `requirements.sdk.outlines.language_provider.structural_search`.\n\n- **Default returns an unsupported-language error.** `outline_only_languages_report_unsupported_structural_search` in `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs` calls `search_structure` on `CustomLanguage`. The `CustomLanguage` impl is visible only as far as `name`, and I did not read the rest of it. I read no sign that it overrides `search_structure`, so the call reaches the trait default. The test asserts that the call returns an error whose message contains \"unsupported\".\n- **Plain text supplies outlining only.** The same test calls `languages::PlainText.search_structure`. It asserts an error whose message contains \"plain-text\".\n- **Operation returns matched byte ranges within the supplied source.**\n  - `bundled_structural_search_returns_matches_and_rejects_bad_rules_and_source` runs a `function_item` rule on Rust source with two functions. It asserts that the returned spans slice out exactly `fn first() {}` and `fn second() {}`.\n  - `bundled_languages_claim_extensions_and_supply_the_same_public_outline` runs a catch-all regex rule for every bundled language. Those are Rust, Python, TypeScript, Tsx, Markdown, Css, Toml and Yaml. For each, it asserts the result is non-empty and that every range is a valid slice of the source.\n- **Failure cases.** The Rust test also asserts an error for an invalid rule object (`{\"unexpected\": true}`) and for unparsable source (`fn {`). The requirement's `Result<Vec<Span>, OutlineError>` signature covers these cases.\n- **Signature.** The tests call the method with `&str` and a `serde_json::Map` and use the `Vec<Span>` and error results. That checks the signature at compile time.\n\nI found no plausible violation that these tests would accept. The requirement only says a language \"can\" supply the capability, so a custom override test is optional.",
